{
  "dom_id": "step-11366",
  "name": "Vertical Translations of the Cosine Function",
  "problem": "Now, recall the graph of the function $y=\\cos x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/8d164a6d3352554394458c10241908cb153f50fe121f1f07879806bd8cf9b106.png)\n\nWe can apply vertical translations to the cosine function. For instance, the graphs of $y=\\cos x{\\color{blue}\\,+\\,2}$ and $y=\\cos x {\\color{red}\\,-\\,1}$ are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/c362c64628ac502ca20ea25cb05f84904598c182551dca5351dc2fb1f199142f.png)\n\nThe $\\color{blue}+\\,2$ moves the curve up by two units, and the $\\color{red}-\\,1$ moves it down by one unit.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
